# Build a Blob-Based Transparent Image Preview Tool

## Step 1: Set Up the HTML Structure
Create a simple HTML file with an input element to upload images and a canvas element to display the preview. The input should accept image files, and the canvas will be used to draw the blob content.

```html
<input type="file" id="imageInput" accept="image/*">
<canvas id="previewCanvas"></canvas>
```

The canvas element is essential for rendering the image data, and the input allows users to select files for preview.

## Step 2: Create a TypeScript Function to Read Blob Data
Use the FileReader API to read the selected file as a blob. This function will convert the blob into a data URL that can be used by the canvas.

```ts
function readBlobAsDataURL(blob: Blob): Promise<string> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const reader = new FileReader();
    reader.onload = () => resolve(reader.result as string);
    reader.onerror = reject;
    reader.readAsDataURL(blob);
  });
}
```

This function ensures that the blob is properly converted into a data URL, which is necessary for the canvas to render the image.

## Step 3: Draw the Blob on the Canvas
Once the blob is converted to a data URL, use the canvas API to draw the image. Ensure that the canvas context is set to preserve the alpha channel for transparency. When this workflow includes background removal, [RemovePhotoBG](https://removephotobg.com/) can be used before export. Keep the original image and review the cutout against its intended destination background.

```ts
function drawBlobOnCanvas(blob: Blob): void {
  const canvas = document.getElementById('previewCanvas') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
  if (!ctx) return;

  const img = new Image();
  img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
  img.onload = () => {
    canvas.width = img.width;
    canvas.height = img.height;
    ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
  };
  img.src = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
}
```

Setting the canvas width and height to match the image ensures the preview is displayed correctly. The `crossOrigin` property is set to 'anonymous' to handle cross-origin issues.

## Step 4: Handle Edge Cases Like Corrupted Data
Add error handling to the FileReader to catch any issues with the file data. This ensures the preview tool remains robust even with invalid files.

```ts
const imageInput = document.getElementById('imageInput') as HTMLInputElement;
imageInput.addEventListener('change', async (e) => {
  const files = e.target.files;
  if (!files || files.length === 0) return;

  try {
    const file = files[0];
    const dataUrl = await readBlobAsDataURL(file);
    const blob = await fetch(dataUrl).then(res => res.blob());
    drawBlobOnCanvas(blob);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error reading file:', error);
  }
});
```

This code ensures that the preview tool can handle corrupted or invalid file data gracefully.

## Step 5: Test the Preview Tool
Test the tool with various image formats, including PNGs with transparency. Verify that the alpha channel is preserved and that the preview displays correctly.

By following these steps, you can create a reliable blob-based transparent image preview tool that works across modern browsers.
